message:
Now I see the issue. In `md_is_inline_link_spec`, after calling `md_is_link_title`, the code does:

```c
while(off < lines[line_index].end  &&  ISWHITESPACE(off))
    off++;
if(off >= lines[line_index].end) {
    line_index++;
    if(line_index >= n_lines)
        return FALSE;
    off = lines[line_index].beg;
}
if(CH(off) != _T(')'))
    goto abort;
```

The issue is that `md_is_link_title` can return TRUE with `off` pointing past the end of the last line (i.e., `off` could be at `lines[line_index].end` or beyond). Then when `md_is_inline_link_spec` checks `off < lines[line_index].end`, if `off` is already past the end, it increments `line_index` and sets `off = lines[line_index].beg`. But if `line_index` was already at the last line, `line_index++` would make it `n_lines`, and then `lines[line_index]` would be out of bounds.

Wait, but there's a check `if(line_index >= n_lines) return FALSE;` before accessing `lines[line_index]`. So that should be safe.

Let me think about this differently. The crash is a heap-buffer-overflow READ 1 in `md_is_link_title`. Let me look at the `md_is_link_title` function more carefully:

```c
md_is_link_title(MD_CTX* ctx, const MD_LINE* lines, MD_SIZE n_lines, OFF beg,
                 OFF* p_end, MD_SIZE* p_beg_line_index, MD_SIZE* p_end_line_index,
                 OFF* p_contents_beg, OFF* p_contents_end)
{
    OFF off = beg;
    CHAR closer_char;
    MD_SIZE line_index = 0;

    /* White space with up to one line break. */
    while(off < lines[line_index].end  &&  ISWHITESPACE(off))
        off++;
    if(off >= lines[line_index].end) {
        line_index++;
        if(line_index >= n_lines)
            return FALSE;
        off = lines[line_index].beg;
    }
    if(off == beg)
        return FALSE;
```

The issue is that `lines[line_index].end` could be beyond `ctx->size`. Let me check how `lines` is constructed. The `lines` array contains line boundaries, and `lines[line_index].end` should be within `ctx->size`. But what if `off` is already past `lines[line_index].end` when `md_is_link_title` is called?

Actually, let me look at the `md_is_link_title` main loop more carefully:

```c
    while(line_index < n_lines) {
        OFF line_end = lines[line_index].end;

        while(off < line_end) {
            if(CH(off) == _T('\\')  &&  off+1 < ctx->size  &&  (ISPUNCT(off+1) || ISNEWLINE(off+1))) {
                off++;
            } else if(CH(off) == closer_char) {
                /* Success. */
                *p_contents_end = off;
                *p_end = off+1;
                *p_end_line_index = line_index;
                return TRUE;
            } else if(closer_char == _T(')')  &&  CH(off) == _T('(')) {
                /* ()-style title cannot contain (unescaped '(')) */
                return FALSE;
            }

            off++;
        }

        line_index++;
    }
```

The inner loop reads `CH(off)` which is `ctx->text[off]`. The loop condition is `off < line_end`, so `off` should be within the line. But `line_end = lines[line_index].end` - is this guaranteed to be within `ctx->size`?

Let me check how `lines` is constructed. Let me look at `md_process_normal_block_contents`:

